# k.server.EmailProvider on the server against the fake SMTP server of
# fakes.toml (docs/design/31-email.md): delivery, the three EmailError
# cases, and what the SMTP server received.

### a message reaches the SMTP server
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Send
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{"to": "ana@example.com", "subject": "Your order", "html": false}
>>> 200
json {"value": "sent"}

### an HTML part makes it multipart
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Send
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{"to": "ben@example.com", "subject": "Your receipt", "html": true}
>>> 200
json {"value": "sent"}

### a 5xx reply is rejected, with the server's text
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Send
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{"to": "reject@example.com", "subject": "Hi", "html": false}
>>> 200
json {"value": "rejected 550 5.1.1 No such user"}

### a 4xx reply is unavailable (try again later)
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Send
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{"to": "defer@example.com", "subject": "Hi", "html": false}
>>> 200
json {"value": "unavailable"}

### a line break in the subject is an invalid message, never sent
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Send
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{"to": "eve@example.com", "subject": "Hi\r\nBcc: everyone@example.com", "html": false}
>>> 200
json {"value": "invalid the subject contains a line break"}

### the inbox has exactly the accepted messages, with MAIL_FROM, copies and Reply-To
POST /mail.MailProtocol/Inbox
content-type: application/json
connect-protocol-version: 1

{}
>>> 200
json {"value": "orders@shop.example -> ana@example.com,copy@shop.example: Your order html=false replyTo=true | orders@shop.example -> ben@example.com,copy@shop.example: Your receipt html=true replyTo=true"}
